    category: "metrics",
    title: "Attribution models — first-touch vs. last-touch",
    content: `Situation: Use when deciding which marketing channel to credit for a sale, and therefore where to allocate future budget.
Strategy: First-touch attribution credits the very first interaction (e.g. the ad that first introduced the brand). Last-touch credits whatever happened right before purchase (e.g. a retargeting ad or discount code at checkout). These can tell very different stories about which channel "works."
Why it works (India): A business that only looks at last-touch data might conclude retargeting is the best channel and cut top-of-funnel spend, without realizing retargeting only had something to retarget because earlier content/ads brought the customer in — cutting the wrong channel based on incomplete attribution.
How to apply: Without sophisticated multi-touch attribution tools, the practical small-business fix is simply asking new customers directly ("how did you hear about us?") to get a rough sense of the full journey, not just the last click.
Expected Metrics: Not a single number; the value is qualitative — understanding that last-touch-only data systematically undercredits awareness-stage channels, and adjusting budget decisions accordingly rather than optimizing purely for last-touch numbers.
Common Mistakes: Cutting top-of-funnel/awareness spend based purely on last-touch attribution data, not realizing it was feeding the channels that get last-touch credit.`,
