    category: "metrics",
    title: "Average Order Value (AOV) and how to move it",
    content: `Situation: Use when looking for growth levers cheaper than acquiring more customers.
Strategy: AOV is total revenue ÷ number of orders. Real levers to increase it: bundling complementary products, a "free shipping above ₹X" threshold set just above current average order (nudging one more item added), or a "customers also bought" upsell at checkout.
Why it works (India): Increasing AOV extracts more value from traffic/customers already being paid to acquire — often cheaper than acquiring entirely new customers, especially relevant when acquisition costs are rising due to competitive ad markets.
How to apply: Calculate current AOV, then set a free-shipping or bundle threshold slightly above it (not far above, which discourages rather than nudges) to test whether it moves the average order size upward.
Expected Metrics: Not a universal number — the relevant benchmark is your own AOV trend before/after introducing a specific lever (bundle, threshold, upsell), tracked over a meaningful sample of orders, not a handful.
Common Mistakes: Focusing exclusively on traffic/acquisition metrics while never testing AOV-increasing levers, which are often cheaper and faster to implement than acquisition improvements.`,
