    title: "A/B testing — what makes a result trustworthy",
    content: `Situation: Use whenever comparing two versions of anything (headline, creative, offer) to decide which performs better — many small-business "tests" declare a winner too early to be trustworthy.
Strategy: A result is only trustworthy with a large enough sample and long enough test duration to rule out random chance. A common mistake: declaring a winner after a handful of conversions each ("version A got 3 sales, version B got 1") — with such small numbers, that's easily just noise.
Why it works (India): This is a general statistical principle, not India-specific — but it directly prevents wasted effort chasing "winners" that were actually random noise, which wastes both time and, if scaled, ad budget on the wrong variant.
How to apply: Don't trust a test result until each version has accumulated at least a few dozen conversions (more for higher-stakes decisions), and let the test run through at least a full week to average out weekday-vs-weekend behavior differences.
Expected Metrics: There's no universal "enough sample size" number that fits every test — but a rough practical floor is several dozen conversions per variant before drawing conclusions, and always across at least one full week.
Common Mistakes: Declaring a winner after single-digit conversion counts per variant; ending a test mid-week without covering both weekday and weekend behavior, which can skew results.`,
